--TEST--
An archive carrying a traversing, absolute-path, symlink or repeated entry is refused when it is read rather than when something extracts it — `rule:security/path-scope-canonicalise-then-prefix`, `rule:core-api/tier-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Four hand-built archives, each one an attack, base64 here because an archive
// is octets and this file is text. What is in each is written beside it, and
// every one of them is a well-formed zip: nothing below is refused for being
// malformed.

// `notes/one.txt`, then `../../etc/passwd`.
bytes $traversing = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// `notes/one.txt`, then `/etc/passwd`.
bytes $absolute = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// `notes/one.txt`, then `notes/link` — an ordinary-looking name whose mode
// carries `S_IFLNK` and whose data is the target, `/etc/passwd`.
bytes $symlink = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// `notes/one.txt` twice, holding different octets.
bytes $twice = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// Listing is a read, so listing is where the refusal happens. There is no
// spelling that hands a program the entry and lets it decide: `entries` is the
// only way to see a name, and it refuses the archive whole.
array<bytes> $hostile = [$traversing, $absolute, $symlink, $twice];
foreach ($hostile as bytes $archive) {
    try {
        Core\Zip::entries($archive);
        echo "an archive was listed\n";
    } catch (ParseError $refused) {
        echo $refused->message, "\n";
    }
}

// And reading the *harmless* entry out of a hostile archive is refused too,
// which is the half PHP's shape gets wrong: `ZipArchive::getFromName` will hand
// over `notes/one.txt` from an archive whose next entry is `../../etc/passwd`,
// and the program that later walks the entries is the one that pays.
try {
    Core\Zip::read($traversing, "notes/one.txt");
    echo "a harmless entry came out of a hostile archive\n";
} catch (ParseError $whole) {
    echo "reading one harmless entry of that archive: refused as well\n";
}

// Every refusal above is a `ParseError` — a diagnostic about what the archive
// is — and never an `IOError`, which is what a caller would retry.
try {
    Core\Zip::entries($symlink);
    echo "unreachable\n";
} catch (IOError $wrong) {
    echo "a hostile archive read as a failing disk\n";
} catch (ParseError $right) {
    echo "a hostile archive is a ParseError, not an IOError\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Zip refuses an entry when the archive is read: it traverses out of the archive with a `..` component: "../../etc/passwd" (rule:security/path-scope-canonicalise-then-prefix). The refusal is the archive's rather than the extraction's, so a program that walks the entries itself has nothing left it could have checked.
Core\Zip refuses an entry when the archive is read: it names an absolute path: "/etc/passwd" (rule:security/path-scope-canonicalise-then-prefix). The refusal is the archive's rather than the extraction's, so a program that walks the entries itself has nothing left it could have checked.
Core\Zip refuses an entry when the archive is read: it is a symlink, whatever its name says: "notes/link" (rule:security/path-scope-canonicalise-then-prefix). The refusal is the archive's rather than the extraction's, so a program that walks the entries itself has nothing left it could have checked.
Core\Zip will not read an entry: the archive names it twice, and which of the two a reader answers is what the attack turns on: "notes/one.txt".
reading one harmless entry of that archive: refused as well
a hostile archive is a ParseError, not an IOError
